13:48:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your experience is real and worth taking seriously—I'm not going to tell you that you didn't observe what you observed. But there are some reasons why your sample might not represent developers as a whole: **Selection effects are strong here:** - Meetups specifically attract people who want to spend their evening talking about tech with strangers. That's already a self-selected group skewing toward people who find tech-focused socializing appealing—which correlates with certain personality traits and interests. Developers who'd rather be at a bar, a gym, or home with family aren't in your sample at all. - Your workplaces are also a sample of specific companies, teams, and hiring pipelines—not a random draw from all developers everywhere. **Confirmation bias is easy to fall into:** - If someone's "into board games" or has a normal hobby, you might not register it as notable. But if someone info-dumps about Warhammer lore for twenty minutes, that sticks in memory. We tend to notice and remember things that confirm a pattern more than things that don't. **"Awkward" is doing a lot of work:** - Some of what reads as social awkwardness might actually be introversion, or just people who are more comfortable with technical topics than small talk. That's different from universal awkwardness—it might just be context-dependent (awkward at a party, fine running a team meeting or mentoring). I'd push back a little: are you also meeting the developers who don't go to meetups, who aren't visibly "nerdy," who blend in fine socially? Almost certainly yes, but you might just not be clocking them as notable examples, because they don't stand out. None of this means your observations are wrong for the population you've actually interacted with—it just means "every developer I've met" is a smaller and more skewed slice of the field than it might feel like.
