13:51:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No—if you add "gets weird in social situations" to the definition, you've actually changed the claim, and that's exactly the distinction that matters. **Introversion alone:** prefers solitude, gets drained by socializing, may not seek out interaction. That's just a preference. Someone can have this and still be perfectly smooth, warm, and competent *when* they are in a social situation—they just don't want to be in one for six hours at a party. Preferring not to do something isn't the same as being bad at it. **"Gets weird in social situations":** this is a different, additional claim—about actual performance/competence during interaction (awkward pauses, missing social cues, over-explaining, not reading the room). This is what "socially awkward" actually means. You can have either one without the other: - Introverted + socially skilled: quiet, doesn't seek out crowds, but perfectly normal and easy to talk to one-on-one. Very common. - Extroverted + socially awkward: loves being around people, seeks it out constantly, but says the wrong thing, overshares, misreads cues. Also common—this is a lot of "that guy who won't stop talking at parties." So no, it's not splitting hairs—it's the difference between "doesn't want to" and "can't." If your actual claim is "many developers I've met seem to struggle *during* interactions, not just avoid them," that's a more specific and falsifiable claim than "they're introverted," and it deserves to be evaluated on its own, not smuggled in under the introversion umbrella. If that's what you've actually observed, I believe you observed it — I just don't think it generalizes to "most developers" for the sample-bias reasons from earlier in this conversation.
