13:52:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely useful data point, and it actually fits my argument rather than contradicting it—let me show you why instead of just asserting it. **I never claimed the "awkward computer guy" thing started as marketing.** I said the opposite: that early computing (70s-80s) really did draw a narrow, specific population—often engineers, academics, hobbyists, military/defense-adjacent people—and that this was a real, observable pattern *before* any branding existed. Your dad's account is consistent with that. What I said came *later* is that companies/media took that real historical kernel and turned it into a durable cultural brand that outlasted the demographic shift. So this isn't new counter-evidence to my position—it's actually the first half of it. **A few things worth flagging about the anecdote itself, though:** - Your dad is describing "computer guys" as an engineer himself—so this is one specialized technical subculture describing another. It's worth asking whether "computer guys were the weird ones" was partly engineers-vs-computer-scientists rivalry/perception (a common dynamic between adjacent technical fields, each seeing the other as the weirder tribe) rather than an objective outside measurement. - In the 70s-80s, "computer guys" was a *much* smaller and more selective population than "software developer" is today—often literally the small number of people who had access to a mainframe or one of the early hobbyist computers. A tiny, self-selected niche being unusually homogeneous in personality is expected and unsurprising; it doesn't tell us much about a global industry of millions today, including people at companies who write CRUD apps, mobile apps, and internal tools with no connection to that original hacker/hobbyist culture at all. - It's still one person's perception, filtered through decades of memory, from one professional and social environment. That's a real, non-nothing piece of evidence—more valuable than a movie trope—but it's still an anecdote, not a survey of the field. So I'd put it this way: your dad's observation is good evidence that the *origin* of the stereotype is old and real, not invented by tech-company marketing. It's much weaker evidence that the stereotype *still accurately describes most people in the field today*, because the field in the 70s-80s and the field now are barely the same population in terms of size, entry points, and diversity.
