13:57:03 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: I need to clarify a common statistical misconception: when we say a group's average is shifted in some direction, that doesn't automatically mean most individuals in that group strongly exhibit that trait. The distributions might overlap substantially with the baseline population, or the shift could be modest despite being statistically significant. This gets at regression to the mean and how overlapping distributions can create situations where group-level patterns don't translate to individual-level prevalence the way people intuitively expect. I should also separate "introverted" from "awkward" — these keep getting conflated in the reasoning, but they're distinct traits. And I'm recognizing this as an ecological fallacy: drawing individual-level conclusions from population-level statistics.
13:57:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a real logical move, and it's worth examining carefully, because it's actually a common statistical trap: **a shifted average doesn't automatically mean "most individuals have the trait strongly."** It depends entirely on how much the distributions overlap, and by how much the average actually shifted. Here's a clean way to see why: men are on average taller than women. That's a true, real average difference. Does that mean "most individual men are tall" or "most individual women are short"? Not really — there's enormous overlap between the two distributions. Plenty of women are taller than plenty of men. The average shift is real, but it doesn't tell you that the categories are cleanly split into "tall people" and "short people" — it tells you the whole curve moved slightly, with huge overlap remaining. Same logic applies here. If developers skew introverted *on average* compared to the general population, that's compatible with: - A modest shift where maybe 55-60% land on the introverted side of the scale, not "practically everyone" - Massive overlap with the general population, meaning plenty of individual developers are as extroverted as the average person off the street - The "on average" shift being driven by a subset who are very strongly introverted, while a large chunk of the group is basically middle-of-the-road So "the field skews introverted on average" and "most individual programmers are noticeably, visibly introverted/awkward" are genuinely different claims, and the first doesn't imply the second — it depends on effect size and distribution shape, which I don't have hard numbers for and haven't seen you or me actually produce in this whole conversation. And this is on top of the separate point from earlier: even granting "most individuals genuinely are introverted," introverted ≠ awkward. So there are two separate gaps between what I've agreed to (a real average tilt toward introversion) and what you're asking me to accept (most individuals are awkward nerds) — a statistical gap (average ≠ most) and a definitional gap (introverted ≠ awkward). Both need to hold for your conclusion to follow, and I don't think either one clears the bar on its own, let alone both together.
